1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,405
This is STAT 331, video 6. The topic is the mean, the variance and the distribution of beta one hat.

2
00:00:08,855 --> 00:00:15,319
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:28,057
Type A: find the mean of an estimator, and say if it is unbiased. Lecture 3 does it for beta one hat. Assignment 1, question 1 b, asks it for beta zero hat.

4
00:00:28,507 --> 00:00:39,515
Type B: derive the variance of an estimator. Lecture 3 does it for beta one hat. Assignment 1, question 1 a, asks it for beta zero hat.

5
00:00:39,965 --> 00:00:45,469
Type C: state the distribution of an estimator, and the assumptions that it needs.

6
00:00:45,919 --> 00:00:56,821
Section 1. Here is our own example. The gray dashed line is the true line, y equals 2 plus 0.5 x. In practice, it is unknown.

7
00:00:57,271 --> 00:01:05,313
We take one sample of 10 points from the model, and fit the least squares line. The slope estimate is 0.600.

8
00:01:05,763 --> 00:01:13,166
A second sample from the same model gives different points. Its slope estimate is 0.359.

9
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:22,469
A third sample gives 0.611. The right panel collects the three estimates. Each sample gives a different estimate.

10
00:01:22,919 --> 00:01:34,631
Now take 2000 samples, and fit a line to each one. The histogram shows the 2000 slope estimates. This is the sampling distribution of beta one hat.

11
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:52,169
The estimates are centred near the true slope, 0.5. Slide 10 asks how to describe this uncertainty. We need the distribution of beta one hat. Standard errors, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests all come from it.

12
00:01:52,619 --> 00:02:15,041
Section 2. Every method card of this video starts with the same step: write the estimator as a weighted sum of the y i. Recall the model: the y i are independent and normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared. Line 1 is the least squares slope from video 4.

13
00:02:15,491 --> 00:02:31,320
The picture uses our own five x values: 1, 3, 5, 7 and 9. x bar is 5. The deviations are minus 4, minus 2, 0, 2 and 4. S x x is the sum of their squares, 40.

14
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:47,493
In line 1, the y bar part goes away. y bar is a constant, so it comes out of the sum. The sum of x i minus x bar is 0. So the top is the sum of y i times x i minus x bar.

15
00:02:47,943 --> 00:03:13,607
Each y i now has its own factor: x i minus x bar, over S x x. Call it w i. Then beta one hat is the sum of w i times y i. For our x values, minus 4 over 40 is minus 0.1. The weights are minus 0.1, minus 0.05, 0, 0.05 and 0.1.

16
00:03:14,057 --> 00:03:29,673
w i uses only the x values. In the model, x is fixed. So the weights are fixed with respect to y. Beta one hat is a linear combination of the y i. This is step 1 of cards A, B and C.

17
00:03:30,123 --> 00:03:42,219
The proofs use three facts about the weights. Fact 1 first. Put in w i. S x x is a constant, so it comes out of the sum. The table shows the five weights.

18
00:03:42,669 --> 00:03:52,930
The deviations x i minus x bar add to 0. So the weights add to 0. In the table, the weights add to 0. This is F 1.

19
00:03:53,380 --> 00:04:03,492
Fact 2: the sum of w i times x i. Put in w i. The top is the sum of x i times x i minus x bar.

20
00:04:03,942 --> 00:04:20,753
Subtract x bar times the sum of x i minus x bar. This is 0, so the top does not change. The top becomes S x x. So the sum is 1. In the table, w i x i adds to 1. This is F 2.

21
00:04:21,203 --> 00:04:31,379
Fact 3: the sum of w i squared. Square the top and the bottom. S x x squared does not depend on i, so it comes out of the sum.

22
00:04:31,829 --> 00:04:45,887
The top is S x x. Cancel one copy. The sum is 1 over S x x. In the table, the squares add to 0.025, which is 1 over 40. This is F 3.

23
00:04:46,337 --> 00:04:53,825
Card A uses F 1 and F 2 in step 4. Card B uses F 1 and F 3 in step 4.

24
00:04:54,275 --> 00:05:11,107
Section 3. Question type A asks for the mean of an estimator. If the mean equals the parameter, the estimator is unbiased. Lecture 3 does it for beta one hat. Assignment 1, question 1 b, asks it for beta zero hat.

25
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:21,541
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: write the estimator as a weighted sum of the y i. The weights use only x, so they are fixed.

26
00:05:21,991 --> 00:05:32,743
Step 2: linearity of expectation. The expected value of the sum of a i y i is the sum of a i times the expected value of y i.

27
00:05:33,193 --> 00:05:44,180
Step 3: put in the expected value of y i, beta zero plus beta one x i. This uses only one assumption: each error has mean 0.

28
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:48,705
Step 4: simplify with the weight facts F 1 and F 2.

29
00:05:49,155 --> 00:05:55,576
Step 5: compare the result with the parameter. If they are equal, the estimator is unbiased.

30
00:05:56,026 --> 00:06:10,554
Here is the derivation of slide 14. Pause the video and try it. We follow card A. Step 1: beta one hat is the sum of w i y i. The picture shows the model and the five weights.

31
00:06:11,004 --> 00:06:26,876
Step 2: take the expected value. The weights are fixed, so linearity gives the sum of w i times the expected value of y i. The expected value is underlined, because the next line puts in its value.

32
00:06:27,326 --> 00:06:37,545
Step 3: the expected value of y i is beta zero plus beta one x i. It is on the true line, because each error has mean 0.

33
00:06:37,995 --> 00:06:46,997
Step 4: simplify. Write out w i: x i minus x bar, over the sum of x j minus x bar squared.

34
00:06:47,447 --> 00:07:04,663
Split the sum into a beta zero part and a beta one part. The beta zero part has the sum of x i minus x bar on top. The picture shows these deviations: minus 4, minus 2, 0, 2 and 4. They add to 0.

35
00:07:05,113 --> 00:07:19,513
So the beta zero part is 0. This is F 1. In the beta one part, we can write x i minus x bar in place of x i, as in F 2. Both tops add to 40 in the picture.

36
00:07:19,963 --> 00:07:26,129
The top is now the same as the bottom. So the fraction is 1, and the mean is beta one.

37
00:07:26,579 --> 00:07:37,011
Step 5: compare. The mean of beta one hat equals beta one. So beta one hat is unbiased. Type C uses this mean in its step 2.

38
00:07:37,461 --> 00:07:48,554
Step 5 of card A says unbiased. Here is what it means. These are the 2000 slope estimates from the simulation. Each sample gives one estimate.

39
00:07:49,004 --> 00:07:52,332
The purple line is the true slope, 0.5.

40
00:07:52,782 --> 00:08:10,169
The average of the 2000 estimates is 0.497. This is very near 0.5. One estimate can be far from beta one. But on average, over many samples, the estimates are correct. This is what unbiased means.

41
00:08:10,619 --> 00:08:27,963
Now Assignment 1, question 1 b. Pause the video and try it. Use the usual model. If the normality assumption does not hold, is beta zero hat unbiased? Explain. We follow card A, and we note each assumption that we use.

42
00:08:28,413 --> 00:08:45,714
Step 1: from video 4, beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar. y bar is the sum of y i over n. Beta one hat is the sum of w i y i. So beta zero hat is linear in the y i.

43
00:08:46,164 --> 00:08:59,135
Step 2: linearity of expectation. x bar is fixed. So the mean is the mean of y bar, minus x bar times the mean of beta one hat. The two means are underlined.

44
00:08:59,585 --> 00:09:14,475
Step 3: the mean of y bar. Put in the expected value of y i, beta zero plus beta one x i. This uses the assumption that each error has mean 0. The list on the right marks it.

45
00:09:14,925 --> 00:09:24,675
Step 4: simplify. The sum of x i over n is x bar. So the mean of y bar is beta zero plus beta one x bar.

46
00:09:25,125 --> 00:09:36,005
The mean of beta one hat is beta one, from slide 14. That proof also used only the expected value of y i, so only the same assumption.

47
00:09:36,455 --> 00:09:47,313
Step 5: put lines 4 and 5 into line 2. Beta zero plus beta one x bar, minus x bar times beta one, is beta zero.

48
00:09:47,763 --> 00:09:59,774
So the answer is yes. The proof uses only linearity of expectation and errors with mean 0. Normality is not used. So beta zero hat is still unbiased.

49
00:10:00,224 --> 00:10:12,192
Here is a check with our own simulation. The errors are not Normal: they are skewed. Their mean is still 0. The true line is still y equals 2 plus 0.5 x.

50
00:10:12,642 --> 00:10:18,551
We take 2000 samples with these errors, and compute beta zero hat for each one.

51
00:10:19,001 --> 00:10:32,633
The true beta zero is 2. The average of the 2000 estimates is 2.010. So the estimates are still centred at beta zero. This agrees with the answer from card A.

52
00:10:33,083 --> 00:10:45,947
Section 4. Question type B asks for the variance of an estimator. Lecture 3 does it for beta one hat. Assignment 1, question 1 a, asks it for beta zero hat.

53
00:10:46,397 --> 00:10:56,915
Method card B has four steps. Step 1 is the same as in card A: write the estimator as a weighted sum of the y i, with fixed weights.

54
00:10:57,365 --> 00:11:10,634
Step 2: the y i are independent, so the variances of the terms add. A constant weight comes out squared. So the variance is the sum of a i squared times the variance of y i.

55
00:11:11,084 --> 00:11:23,223
Step 3: each y i has the same variance, sigma squared. The picture shows the same spread at each x. So the variance is sigma squared times the sum of a i squared.

56
00:11:23,673 --> 00:11:29,411
Step 4: simplify the sum of a i squared with the weight facts F 1 and F 3.

57
00:11:29,861 --> 00:11:42,277
Here is the derivation of slides 19 to 23. Pause the video and try it. We follow card B. Step 1: beta one hat is the sum of w i y i.

58
00:11:42,727 --> 00:11:55,975
Step 2: the y i are independent, so the variances of the terms add. Each weight comes out squared. The variance of y i is underlined, because the next line puts in its value.

59
00:11:56,425 --> 00:12:11,913
Step 3: each y i has variance sigma squared. The picture shows the same spread at each x. So the variance is sigma squared times the sum of w i squared. This is the first line of slide 19.

60
00:12:12,363 --> 00:12:29,003
Step 4: simplify. Write out w i: x i minus x bar, over S x x. S x x is the sum of x j minus x bar squared. The picture shows w i squared for our five x values.

61
00:12:29,453 --> 00:12:33,763
Square the top and the bottom. The bottom is S x x squared.

62
00:12:34,213 --> 00:12:42,639
The bottom does not depend on i. So it comes out of the sum. The top is the sum of x i minus x bar squared.

63
00:12:43,089 --> 00:12:58,321
The top is S x x. Cancel one copy. The variance is sigma squared over S x x. This is F 3. In the picture, the squares add to 0.025, which is 1 over 40.

64
00:12:58,771 --> 00:13:05,939
So the variance of beta one hat is sigma squared over S x x. Type C uses it in step 3.

65
00:13:06,389 --> 00:13:22,624
The result of card B tells us how to get a precise slope. On the left, the 10 x values are close together. S x x is 16.3. We draw 15 samples from the true line, and fit 15 lines. The lines turn a lot.

66
00:13:23,074 --> 00:13:33,975
On the right, the 10 x values are spread out, from 1 to 10. S x x is 82.5. The 15 fitted lines stay close to the true line.

67
00:13:34,425 --> 00:13:56,697
Here are the two Normal densities of beta one hat. The standard deviation is sigma over the square root of S x x. 1 over the square root of 16.3 is 0.248. 1 over the square root of 82.5 is 0.110. Both are centred at the true slope.

68
00:13:57,147 --> 00:14:09,009
So the variance is small when sigma squared is small, or when S x x is large. S x x grows when we add points, or when the x values are more spread out.

69
00:14:09,459 --> 00:14:22,621
Now Assignment 1, question 1 a. Pause the video and try it. Use the usual model. Derive the variance of beta zero hat. We follow card B. Step 1 needs some work here.

70
00:14:23,071 --> 00:14:28,639
From video 4, beta zero hat is y bar minus beta one hat x bar.

71
00:14:29,089 --> 00:14:41,911
y bar is the sum of 1 over n times y i, in purple. So each y i has the weight 1 over n. For our five x values, n is 5, so each weight is 0.2.

72
00:14:42,361 --> 00:15:03,417
The second part, in orange, is minus x bar times the sum of w i y i. So its weights are minus x bar times w i. With x bar equal to 5, they are 0.5, 0.25, 0, minus 0.25 and minus 0.5.

73
00:15:03,867 --> 00:15:22,917
Add the two weights of each y i. The result is c i, 1 over n minus x bar w i. For our x values, the c i are 0.7, 0.45, 0.2, minus 0.05 and minus 0.3.

74
00:15:23,367 --> 00:15:33,074
The c i use only x, so they are fixed. So beta zero hat is a weighted sum of the y i, and the other steps of card B apply.

75
00:15:33,524 --> 00:15:49,887
Step 1 is done: beta zero hat is the sum of c i y i. For our x values, the c i are 0.7, 0.45, 0.2, minus 0.05 and minus 0.3.

76
00:15:50,337 --> 00:15:56,587
Step 2: the y i are independent, so the variances add. Each c i comes out squared.

77
00:15:57,037 --> 00:16:05,229
Step 3: each y i has variance sigma squared. So the variance is sigma squared times the sum of c i squared.

78
00:16:05,679 --> 00:16:23,151
Step 4: simplify. Expand one square. c i squared is 1 over n squared, minus 2 x bar over n times w i, plus x bar squared times w i squared. The picture shows c i squared for our x values.

79
00:16:23,601 --> 00:16:32,412
Add over i. The first term appears n times, so it gives n over n squared. The constants come out of the other two sums.

80
00:16:32,862 --> 00:16:51,827
Use F 1: the weights add to 0. Use F 3: the squared weights add to 1 over S x x. So the sum of c i squared is 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x. In the picture, the squares add to 0.825.

81
00:16:52,277 --> 00:17:13,205
Put line 6 into line 3. The variance of beta zero hat is sigma squared times 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x. Check: 1 over 5 plus 25 over 40 is 0.2 plus 0.625, which is 0.825.

82
00:17:13,655 --> 00:17:18,754
This is the answer to question 1 a, and the variance on slide 24.

83
00:17:19,204 --> 00:17:33,860
Section 5. Question type C asks for the distribution of an estimator, and the assumptions that each part needs. Slide 24 states the distributions of beta one hat and beta zero hat.

84
00:17:34,310 --> 00:17:48,539
Step 1 of method card C: the estimator is a weighted sum of independent Normal y i. Use this rule from STAT 230. A linear combination of independent Normals is Normal.

85
00:17:48,989 --> 00:17:51,464
Step 2: the mean comes from card A.

86
00:17:51,914 --> 00:17:54,794
Step 3: the variance comes from card B.

87
00:17:55,244 --> 00:18:03,436
Step 4: write the Normal distribution with this mean and this variance. Then name the assumptions that each part uses.

88
00:18:03,886 --> 00:18:19,267
Here are slides 12, 13 and 24. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: beta one hat is the sum of w i y i. The y i are independent Normals. The picture shows them.

89
00:18:19,717 --> 00:18:25,392
Beta one hat multiplies each y i by its fixed weight, and adds the products.

90
00:18:25,842 --> 00:18:41,778
So beta one hat is Normal. The rule gives its mean, the sum of w i times beta zero plus beta one x i, and its variance, sigma squared times the sum of w i squared. This is slide 13.

91
00:18:42,228 --> 00:18:45,449
Step 2: card A showed that the mean is beta one.

92
00:18:45,899 --> 00:18:50,998
Step 3: card B showed that the variance is sigma squared over S x x.

93
00:18:51,448 --> 00:19:07,939
So beta one hat is Normal, with mean beta one and variance sigma squared over S x x. Its standard deviation is sigma over the square root of S x x. Video 7 uses this result for the confidence interval.

94
00:19:08,389 --> 00:19:21,338
Check the result with our own simulation. Here x is 1 to 10, and sigma is 1. The green curve is the Normal density of the formula. Its mean is beta one, 0.5.

95
00:19:21,788 --> 00:19:32,412
Its standard deviation is sigma over the square root of S x x. 1 over the square root of 82.5 is 0.110.

96
00:19:32,862 --> 00:19:39,262
The blue bars are the 2000 simulated estimates. They follow the green Normal curve.

97
00:19:39,712 --> 00:19:47,435
So the simulation agrees with the formula: Normal, mean beta one, variance sigma squared over S x x.

98
00:19:47,885 --> 00:20:08,194
Slide 24 asks: what about beta zero hat? Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: from Assignment 1, question 1 a, beta zero hat is the sum of c i y i with fixed c i. So it is Normal. The picture shows 20 samples and their 20 fitted lines.

99
00:20:08,644 --> 00:20:17,732
Step 2: card A gave the mean, beta zero. This was Assignment 1, question 1 b. The lines are centred on the true line.

100
00:20:18,182 --> 00:20:38,683
Step 3: card B gave the variance, sigma squared times 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x. The 1 over n part: every least squares line goes through x bar, y bar. At x bar, the spread comes only from y bar, with variance sigma squared over n.

101
00:20:39,133 --> 00:20:57,245
The x bar squared over S x x part: beta zero hat is the height of the line at x equals 0. Here 0 is 5.5 units from x bar. An error in the slope moves the line more when we go far from x bar. So the intercepts spread out more.

102
00:20:57,695 --> 00:21:10,922
Step 4: beta zero hat is Normal, with mean beta zero, and variance sigma squared times 1 over n plus x bar squared over S x x. This is slide 24.

103
00:21:11,372 --> 00:21:21,612
Step 4 of card C also names the assumptions. Unbiasedness needs only linearity and errors with mean 0. Card A used nothing else.

104
00:21:22,062 --> 00:21:31,577
The variance, sigma squared over S x x, also needs independence and equal variance. Card B used them in steps 2 and 3.

105
00:21:32,027 --> 00:21:39,728
The Normal distribution needs all four assumptions, also normality. Card C used normality in step 1.

106
00:21:40,178 --> 00:21:57,117
The same holds for beta zero hat. So in Assignment 1, question 1 b, beta zero hat stays unbiased without normality. Question 1 c asks about independence. Video 7 answers it, after the confidence interval.

107
00:21:57,567 --> 00:22:16,297
To recap. Every card starts with a weighted sum of the y i, and uses the three weight facts. Card A finds the mean: linearity of expectation, the mean of y i, the weight facts, and a comparison. Both estimators are unbiased, also without normality.

108
00:22:16,747 --> 00:22:27,521
Card B finds the variance: independence, equal variance, and the weight facts. The variance of beta one hat is sigma squared over S x x.

109
00:22:27,971 --> 00:22:39,277
Card C gives the distribution: Normal, with the mean of card A and the variance of card B. The next video uses it to build confidence intervals for beta one.
